Andrew Kerr <andrewkerruk@spamguard.yahoo.co.uk> writes:
> [ client which could prevent or prompt for obviously-stupid actions ]
> [ example: picking up a cockatrice corpse whilst ungloved ]
> a) Unspoiled newbie. Painful learning experience. Not fun.

If our hypothetical player, newbie or otherwise, finds painful
learning experiences non-fun, then nethack is not the game for
them. If a game provides no painful learning experiences, then the
game in question is not nethack, and is thus un-fun for (at least) an
important subset of nethack players.

In other words, part of nethack's considerable charm lies in the fact
that, in contrast to most other games, it makes ignorance and screwups
*hurt*. Break that, and you break the game for masochists such as
myself.

> b) Pressed wrong key. D'oh! The UI _should_ be helping to avoiding 
> these.

See above. The UI should make it easy for me to command the game. It
should make no value judgments as to which actions are wise or unwise.
(Exception: Commonly-used commands should take as few keystrokes as
possible; seldom-used commands may be relegated to #-commands.)

If there were a reliable platform-independent way to clearly highlight
peaceful and tame creatures, I'd say that prompting before attacking
these would be on the borderline of excessive UI friendliness.

Think before you type. Every keystroke, every time, or eat the
consequences.

> c) Brain malfunction. Forgot not wearing gloves. A UI could easily 
> highlight current wielded weapon and worn items. The existing Qt 
> interface does.

There are already perfectly good '[' and ')' commands which take a
keystroke or two and no turns. If you choose not to avail yourself of
these options, it is hardly the game's fault when you come to grief.

> In short, having the client prevent insta-YASDs is not such a big deal. 

I beg to differ.

Nethack is all about consistently paying attention to details, and
thinking before you act. The game you're proposing is about niceness
and fair play, and (not coincidentally) entirely un-nethackish. IMHFO
of course.

May as well have a dancing paper clip that pops up at random times and
says things like "You seem to be trying to kill Juiblex while trapped
inside him. Let me show you how to zap your wand of digging..."

> [...] the message "Common sense prevents you from wielding the
> cockatrice corpse" is not very Nethackish. A (y/n) prompt would be
> more in keeping.

How are these two things any different? Who are the set of people who'll
say 'y' at the prompt, apart from tournament players trying for most
different deaths?

Really. This is nethack we're talking about, not Cuddle Bear's
Adventures in the Non-Threatening Forest.


